diff --git a/collab/QUESTIONS.md b/collab/QUESTIONS.md
index 2b2be3fd..d460c9de 100644
--- a/collab/QUESTIONS.md
+++ b/collab/QUESTIONS.md
@@ -1444,3 +1444,4 @@ One line per product decision made while building, newest last.
 - Product owner, 2026-10-15 (board 1.34, what is found counts as left; three riders): where Arbor is the reason that a window lies mostly out of view (another window of its app sits where Arbor puts windows out of view), Arbor fetches it, once, whether it opened or was found at a start; not while that sibling is in the Dock or hidden by its person. A floating window that went out with the view is given back by Quit, pause and Float All exactly where its person left it, however little shows: a Quit that tidies moves what it never took. What stays gets the note a hand leaves, so that no later ⌃⌥0, dock or closing pulls it in. And displays that changed while Arbor was off: the windows macOS moved count as left. Arbor moves nothing at a start that it did not itself put away.
 - Product owner, 2026-10-15 (the gate's rounds of input; read in the pull request): an event that never arrives must be judged. A round says what it posted, by kind; fewer arrivals than were posted is a `FAIL` with Arbor and the instrument's own without; a sabotage drops one. It is what macOS does behind a tap gone wrong: dropped, never late. And a kind counts as judged in a journey only if both pairs have their ten events: the round before the last Quit, with an arrangement at work, is the more telling of the two.
 - Product owner, 2026-10-15 (board 1.46; which question turns): only a trial tap. On the road where the right goes first it is refused 0 to 33 ms after the first dropped event, at about a third of a millisecond a call, and in 10,169 calls it never took longer than 81 ms to answer. The flag, "is my tap still enabled?" and both preflights never turn there, and the callback is told nothing. So the clock's question is the trial tap: "Arbor goes by the tap, not by the flag", confirmed from the other side. At that price a beat of 50 ms is two thirds of a per cent of a core: too much for all day, nothing if taps are in only while Arbor's modifiers are down (IDEAS I16).
+- (Q16, Q20, Q21; the floating half of "a frame a person gives a window while Arbor is paused, or not running, is its own", as it is read by whoever built it, 2026-10-15) Two rules. **A display that is back fetches no floating window that is in view**, whoever ended the last run: the window floats on the display it is on, with its role. Overruled with it: "floating windows of a display that is back at launch go back to it", which a test held. **A floating window that somebody has touched since Arbor let go of it has the frame it is found at for its own**, and whether somebody has is known, not guessed: a quit and a pause write down where they left each window (`leftAt` in state.json; nothing else writes it, and the next start takes it out first thing). It answers F48's old note "Arbor cannot tell a window the user moved while it was not running from one a crash left": after a quit or a pause it can, window by window. Found where it was left, what is remembered stands, so a window whose app hung or did not obey when Arbor let go is not taken to be as small as Arbor had squeezed it; moved only, it keeps its own size (a move is not a resize). After a crash or without the permission nothing is written, and what is saved stands as before: to take what is found there broke rule 5 for good (tried: 23 more walks in 800 lost a size). **Docked windows when the displays differ from those at the quit** (Q16 says it): on a display that is back they are docked again, one move each; those of a display that is missing float where they are found, no frame set, and go back with it. Not cured here: what a person moves or resizes after a crash is still thrown back by the next Show Everything, and a window kept in view that was made smaller then is blown up at the launch; a floating window still listed with a display that stays away at the start goes to that display when it is plugged in later in the run; where `summon` remembers a window; after a crash with a window in its corner, a display that stands or is plugged in beside that corner, so that the window shows there, makes the corner its place.
